# LACSFC

Learning Adjacency-Continuous Space-Filling Curves for Efficient Multidimensional Range Queries.

Workload-driven construction of **adjacency-continuous (AC)** Hamiltonian paths on
d-dimensional grids, via N-d Cover-and-Merge with exact `ΔΦ` edge weights and a
maximum-weight spanning tree (MWST). See `docs/technical_plan.md` for the full
technical plan, theorems and experiment protocol.

## Layout

```
lacsfc/
  docs/technical_plan.md   technical plan (theory, algorithms, experiments)
  src/lacsfc/
    grid.py        shape validation, parity masks, coordinate helpers
    gray.py        reflected Gray block cycle, facet-edge assignment (Lemma L-σ′)
    weights.py     co-coverage J(u, u+e_k) via d-dim difference arrays
    construct.py   initial cycles, merge squares, ΔΦ, MWST, Cover-and-Merge, path
    metrics.py     Φ, segment counts, UB0 upper bound
    baselines.py   snake (AC), Z-order, Hilbert (Skilling)
    workloads.py   synthetic range-query generators
  tests/           E0 correctness tests
```

## Setup

```bash
conda activate lacsfc
pip install -e ".[dev]"
pytest -q
```

## Minimal usage

```python
import numpy as np
from lacsfc import build_curve, pair_weights, random_boxes

shape = (16, 16, 8)
rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
lo, hi = random_boxes(shape, 2000, rng)
J = pair_weights(shape, lo, hi)
curve = build_curve(shape, J)
curve.order   # flat cell indices along the AC path, order[0] == 0
curve.rank    # rank[cell] = position along the path
```

Reference: the 2D merge rule follows the NSFC code in `../basic/context.py`
(`apply_mst`, `calc_weight`), which is used only as a 2D differential-test oracle.
